If $\ell \parallel m$ and a transversal cuts both:

$\bullet$ corresponding angles are equal;

$\bullet$ alternate interior angles are equal;

$\bullet$ alternate exterior angles are equal;

$\bullet$ co-interior (same-side interior) angles add to $180^{\circ}$.

Practical shortcut: in the whole figure every angle is either equal to the one you were given or is its supplement. Decide which by looking at the picture -- angles that \emph{look} the same size are the same size.

If two similar figures have corresponding lengths in the ratio $k$ (so every length in the second is $k$ times the matching length in the first), then

$\bullet$ every length -- side, perimeter, height, median -- is in the ratio $k$;

$\bullet$ every area is in the ratio $k^{2}$;

$\bullet$ (for solids) every volume is in the ratio $k^{3}$.

Running it backwards: if the areas are in the ratio $9:16$, the lengths are in the ratio $3:4$.

